windows: trap writes to generated code a host page at a time

A program that generates code at run time (a regular-expression or script engine's compiler)
writes it into memory that is writable and executable, runs it, and later writes other code
to the same place. On x86 it has nothing else to do, so the emulator has to notice the write
by itself. FEX does that by taking the write permission off a page it has translated code
from (InvalidationTracker, RWXIntervals): the write faults, the page's translations are
dropped, and the page is made writable again.

That is done 4K page by 4K page, which is what Windows programs see. The host can only change
the permission of a page of its own, and on Apple silicon those are 16K, four 4K pages. Wine
gives a host page every permission any of its four pages has (get_host_page_vprot). So:

- taking the write permission off one 4K page did nothing while a neighbour in the same host
  page was writable, which is the usual case inside a block of generated code: the write did
  not fault and the old translation went on running;
- making one 4K page writable again made all four writable, so the other three lost their trap
  while their translations were kept.

HoYoPlay crashed this way (issue 128): Qt compiles its regular expressions to machine code
(PCRE2), a second expression's code was written where a first one's had been, the first one's
translation ran, left the stack pointer 0x110 bytes off and returned to address 0. It happened
when a start folder was passed to the folder dialog, which is why locating a second game
crashed and the first did not.

Now pages are trapped, untrapped and have their translations dropped a host page at a time
(TrapGranule): 16K when Wine says the host is macOS, 4K as before otherwise.

- ReprotectRWXIntervals and BeginUntrackedWriteLocked work on whole host pages.
- A write fault drops the translations of the whole host page and gives all of its trapped
  pages their write permission back.
- When memory becomes writable, the translations from the rest of the host pages it starts
  and ends in are dropped: those pages can be written without a fault from then on.
- Writable code that shares a host page with ordinary writable data cannot be trapped at all.
  QueryExecutableRange looks at the neighbours of the first and last host page of a writable
  range (the pages in between are the range's own) and reports such a piece with
  WritesUntrapped; the decoder then marks its blocks ForceFullSMCDetection, which checks the
  bytes before each run (see 0013 for why that needed a fix on ARM64EC).

FEX_SMCGRANULE=<bytes> sets the unit by hand; 4096 is the old behaviour, to rule the change in
or out. Checked with tests/runtime/code-rewrite-silent.c, x64 and 32-bit: it fails with
FEX_SMCGRANULE=4096 (thousands of calls run old code) and passes without.

Local patch only: FEX does not take AI-written code.

